  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Slow down to sentence speed', note: 'Close reading (from the New Critics, 1940s) means treating every word as a choice. Why THIS word, not a near-synonym? What is repeated? What is conspicuously absent?' },
    { math: '2 · Diction sets the register', note: 'Word choice signals class, era, tone: "kids", "children", "offspring", "brats" — four attitudes in four nouns. The first analytical question of any text is the diction question.' },
    { math: '3 · Imagery makes the abstract concrete', note: 'When a poem about grief keeps showing weather, the weather IS the argument. Track image clusters: they carry the emotional logic the surface sentences don\'t state.' },
    { math: '4 · The sentence is the argument', note: 'Long balanced sentences vs short fractured ones — syntax performs meaning. Faulkner\'s endurance-length sentences enact endurance; Hemingway\'s staccato enacts restraint. Form argues too.' },
