<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/intermediate/close-reading">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Close reading is literature's fundamental method: <strong>analysing a text at
    word-and-sentence level to see how it makes its effects</strong>. New Critics (Brooks,
    Warren, Empson) built the discipline in the 1930s–40s on a radical claim — the text itself
    contains the evidence, and careful reading outranks biographical gossip or guessed intentions.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four instruments: diction, imagery, syntax, repetition" widget="equationStep" config={closeCfg} />

  <h2>Worked example: the method in miniature</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Take Dickinson: "Because I could not stop for Death — / He kindly stopped for me —"</strong> The diction ("kindly") is the shock — Death as courteous suitor, not horror. The dashes enact hesitation; the carriage ride structures the poem as a journey. Every claim below the surface comes from the words on it.</li>
    <li><strong>Repetition is never accidental:</strong> anaphora ("I have a dream…"), refrains, repeated images — track what returns and how it changes on return.</li>
    <li><strong>Connotation over denotation:</strong> "slender" and "scrawny" denote similar size; they connote opposite judgments. Close reading reads the connotations.</li>
    <li><strong>Then zoom out:</strong> close reading serves interpretation — the pattern you find locally becomes the claim you make globally.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brooks, The Well Wrought Urn (1947); Empson, Seven Types of Ambiguity (1930); the Dickinson poem itself.</p>
</TopicPage>
